1
00:00:16,057 --> 00:00:18,226
SEOUL

2
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:28,569
Give us a chance!
C'mon, we'll buy you drinks.

3
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:30,320
- Here, that way.
- Where you going?

4
00:00:30,321 --> 00:00:32,156
I need a shot.

5
00:00:32,157 --> 00:00:34,867
- Hey, hey, hey. She's just a kid.
- Huh, she's pretty hot.

6
00:00:34,868 --> 00:00:37,786
You wanna get arrested?
She's a baby. Let's go.

7
00:00:37,787 --> 00:00:39,873
Trust me, you don't want that jail bait.

8
00:00:47,172 --> 00:00:48,797
TURN RIGHT AT
HOTEL 3-WAY INTERSECTION

9
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:51,301
PARKING LOT ELECTRICAL PANEL
ACROSS FROM JANGMI BUILDING - K

10
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:08,229
THE BLUE ROAD

11
00:03:11,316 --> 00:03:13,693
BODY FOUND
AHEAD OF OLYMPIC OPENING

12
00:03:15,445 --> 00:03:18,907
He's upped his game.
More brutal, more meticulous.

13
00:03:19,407 --> 00:03:21,534
Did he evolve of his own accord?

14
00:03:23,328 --> 00:03:25,245
- Let's do it.
- <i>He's approaching the goal.</i>

15
00:03:25,246 --> 00:03:26,623
Come on, shoot it!

16
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:32,003
Come on!

17
00:03:32,795 --> 00:03:36,757
<i>The ball hits the goalpost.
And no goal for Japan.</i>

18
00:03:36,758 --> 00:03:39,927
Man, this sucks!
I've got money riding on this game.

19
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:42,680
It's okay. Come on. Come on, get it back.

20
00:03:43,556 --> 00:03:46,059
We lose to Japan,
we'll have to commit <i>seppuku.</i>

21
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:52,190
What makes you say that?

22
00:03:57,612 --> 00:03:59,948
Because those bastards
are itching to kill us.

23
00:04:04,911 --> 00:04:07,372
- This way! Here, I know a spot.
- C'mon! Hurry!

24
00:04:09,332 --> 00:04:10,541
Hey.

25
00:04:14,462 --> 00:04:15,505
Hello.

26
00:04:22,762 --> 00:04:24,264
Trying to intimidate me?

27
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:27,976
You must be the yakuza.

28
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:30,687
Watch your mouth.

29
00:04:31,688 --> 00:04:33,231
You're Inspector Kim Seok.

30
00:04:34,232 --> 00:04:36,233
Let's make this easy.
We've something to discuss.

31
00:04:36,234 --> 00:04:38,611
Not until
you identify yourselves properly.

32
00:04:41,406 --> 00:04:43,074
You need an eyewitness.

33
00:04:49,872 --> 00:04:51,374
And you haven't found one yet.

34
00:05:10,518 --> 00:05:17,025
TAMPOPO PACHINKO

35
00:05:36,419 --> 00:05:37,962
- Welcome back.
- Hello.

36
00:05:54,103 --> 00:05:55,229
Sit down.

37
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:02,569
One of our men
overheard this Chinese junkie

38
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:05,656
running his mouth
at some bulgogi restaurant in Shibuya.

39
00:06:06,491 --> 00:06:08,785
Claimed he was a witness to the crime.

40
00:06:09,369 --> 00:06:10,953
Says he saw the whole thing.

41
00:06:13,998 --> 00:06:17,085
So you thought you'd kidnap him,
make threats, torture him?

42
00:06:17,752 --> 00:06:19,003
Now, now, detective.

43
00:06:21,923 --> 00:06:23,883
Don't look a gift horse in the mouth.

44
00:06:24,926 --> 00:06:27,553
This man's got answers.
Show some gratitude.

45
00:06:30,556 --> 00:06:31,891
Go on.

46
00:06:32,642 --> 00:06:35,311
Let's go. From the beginning.

47
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:40,691
That abandoned building,

48
00:06:41,192 --> 00:06:44,737
Shinjuku junkies use it all the time
as a meetup spot.

49
00:06:45,363 --> 00:06:49,408
The abandoned building
in Shinjuku is often used by junkies

50
00:06:49,409 --> 00:06:51,285
as a hiding spot for them to shoot up.

51
00:06:51,911 --> 00:06:53,788
Did you know Miyashita Aki?

52
00:06:55,039 --> 00:06:55,915
The dead girl?

53
00:06:56,499 --> 00:06:58,167
You knew her, didn't you?

54
00:06:58,668 --> 00:07:00,460
I did.

55
00:07:00,461 --> 00:07:04,048
We'd get high, hang out together.
Only a few times.

56
00:07:04,966 --> 00:07:07,509
Then she said
that she could find it for less.

57
00:07:07,510 --> 00:07:09,720
I didn't see her all that much after that.

58
00:07:16,644 --> 00:07:17,687
<i>That day...</i>

59
00:08:02,899 --> 00:08:06,902
<i>Her arms and legs
were completely twisted around.</i>

60
00:08:06,903 --> 00:08:08,571
<i>What I saw there wasn't human.</i>

61
00:08:10,239 --> 00:08:12,157
A beast, a creature.

62
00:08:12,158 --> 00:08:13,367
Not human.

63
00:08:16,329 --> 00:08:19,457
It was a monster. It was terrifying!

64
00:08:21,375 --> 00:08:22,585
It was a tiger!

65
00:08:25,296 --> 00:08:26,756
A tiger creature!

66
00:08:31,427 --> 00:08:32,345
Get up.

67
00:08:37,433 --> 00:08:41,854
The Japanese police
are determined to pin this case on us.

68
00:08:42,355 --> 00:08:44,273
It's becoming a pain in the ass.

69
00:08:46,692 --> 00:08:48,528
This time it's for free.

70
00:08:50,321 --> 00:08:53,491
But next time,
we'll be expecting something in return.

71
00:09:04,126 --> 00:09:06,379
You pull a stunt like this one more time...

72
00:09:07,713 --> 00:09:09,840
...and I'll slap the handcuffs
on you myself.

73
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:14,261
ITO MAKOTO

74
00:09:14,262 --> 00:09:16,639
My boss wanted to make sure that you knew.

75
00:09:17,557 --> 00:09:20,308
Think of him as an ally in times of need.

76
00:09:20,309 --> 00:09:24,855
SEO HYUN-TAE

77
00:09:42,582 --> 00:09:44,750
<i>Back in the '80s, in Korea...</i>

78
00:09:48,170 --> 00:09:51,007
they found a body, all twisted,
the limbs all broken.

79
00:09:55,261 --> 00:09:57,054
That case wasn't the first either.

80
00:09:59,515 --> 00:10:02,893
<i>The original killer has indeed returned.</i>

81
00:10:16,782 --> 00:10:18,993
Things are getting interesting.

82
00:10:28,836 --> 00:10:31,213
So our eyewitness was a junkie after all.

83
00:10:41,807 --> 00:10:45,852
The perp lured Miyashita Aki over to him
by promising drugs for cheaper.

84
00:10:45,853 --> 00:10:46,896
And...

85
00:10:52,276 --> 00:10:53,235
What is it?

86
00:10:54,695 --> 00:10:57,489
Is this your plan,
to keep going rogue like this?

87
00:10:57,490 --> 00:11:00,326
You told me about that old case in Korea.

88
00:11:00,826 --> 00:11:05,206
Wasn't that you suggesting
that you and I work together on this?

89
00:11:06,791 --> 00:11:07,792
I suppose.

90
00:11:08,292 --> 00:11:11,879
Well, in that case,
we do everything on this together.

91
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:13,255
Everything.

92
00:11:16,008 --> 00:11:16,884
Right.

93
00:11:17,593 --> 00:11:18,636
Understood.

94
00:11:24,225 --> 00:11:25,476
You serious?

95
00:11:37,113 --> 00:11:38,072
Here.

96
00:11:40,282 --> 00:11:42,409
The junkie, he said he saw a tiger.

97
00:11:42,410 --> 00:11:44,494
SEO HYUN-TAE

98
00:11:44,495 --> 00:11:47,372
And you got this from one of Ito's guys?

99
00:11:47,373 --> 00:11:49,708
What, is he famous? This guy?

100
00:11:49,709 --> 00:11:51,710
His Korean name is Seo Hyun-tae.

101
00:11:51,711 --> 00:11:54,337
He basically runs
the Tokyo pachinko industry.

102
00:11:54,338 --> 00:11:57,716
However, not one person in Japan
has actually seen him.

103
00:11:57,717 --> 00:12:01,345
He probably...
likes to stay out of the spotlight.

104
00:12:02,805 --> 00:12:05,516
- Hmm.
- So, she's gotta be his second in command.

105
00:12:06,434 --> 00:12:08,351
It's all making sense.

106
00:12:08,352 --> 00:12:11,521
There are rumors that Ito
is doing business in the drug trade too,

107
00:12:11,522 --> 00:12:13,274
along with the pachinko stuff.

108
00:12:14,108 --> 00:12:15,151
No proof though.

109
00:12:17,862 --> 00:12:19,946
That Chinese guy was supplying Speed K.

110
00:12:19,947 --> 00:12:23,408
It's the only way they could have,
uh, caught him for you so fast.

111
00:12:23,409 --> 00:12:24,493
Speed K.

112
00:12:25,411 --> 00:12:29,956
In any case, it's not like
she gave us any real specifics.

113
00:12:29,957 --> 00:12:34,170
Tell me about that Korean case.
You know, the one from the '80s.

114
00:12:36,672 --> 00:12:39,132
Hey, look at that.
A welcome committee this early?

115
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:41,093
Have you gone insane?

116
00:12:42,553 --> 00:12:45,680
Did you really think
that we wouldn't find out eventually?

117
00:12:45,681 --> 00:12:48,851
Pack your bags
and get on the next flight back to Korea.

118
00:12:53,647 --> 00:12:56,274
Don't you ever show up
on our doorstep again.

119
00:12:56,275 --> 00:12:57,777
Hey, to be continued.

120
00:13:01,363 --> 00:13:02,281
Huh?

121
00:13:04,658 --> 00:13:05,742
I can't believe it.

122
00:13:05,743 --> 00:13:08,453
He lied to both the Japanese
and the Korean police?

123
00:13:08,454 --> 00:13:10,580
How do you even forge
an official document like that

124
00:13:10,581 --> 00:13:12,541
in such a short amount of time?

125
00:13:13,125 --> 00:13:15,669
It's no laughing matter.
This affects all of us.

126
00:13:16,170 --> 00:13:18,464
Right. Forgive me, sir.

127
00:13:23,135 --> 00:13:24,720
What's going to happen to him?

128
00:13:25,346 --> 00:13:29,682
It looks like he issued an apology,
and the higher-ups quietly accepted it.

129
00:13:29,683 --> 00:13:33,187
So, then they'll probably just
order him to leave.

130
00:13:33,938 --> 00:13:36,273
The leniency is unbelievable.

131
00:13:36,816 --> 00:13:38,484
Call that leniency?

132
00:13:39,151 --> 00:13:42,529
If word of this gets out, this whole
agency will be a laughingstock.

133
00:13:42,530 --> 00:13:45,282
Inose. Come on. Don't say that.

134
00:13:45,783 --> 00:13:46,784
No jinxing.

135
00:13:48,202 --> 00:13:50,621
You've gotta be
at least a little angry with him.

136
00:14:22,611 --> 00:14:25,447
And after all that, still so damn smug.

137
00:14:27,199 --> 00:14:29,618
I wonder what's really going on.

138
00:14:34,373 --> 00:14:35,291
What is it?

139
00:14:36,083 --> 00:14:37,626
What are you up to now?

140
00:14:38,502 --> 00:14:40,504
Don't any of you find it bizarre?

141
00:14:42,506 --> 00:14:47,052
Throwing away your career, your badge,
and all for the sake of solving a case.

142
00:14:51,223 --> 00:14:53,058
Wow. Talk about crazy.

143
00:14:53,642 --> 00:14:55,184
It's like they're the same guy.

144
00:14:55,185 --> 00:14:56,896
I've never seen this happen before.

145
00:14:57,813 --> 00:15:01,065
There is no room around here
for the likes of you.

146
00:15:01,066 --> 00:15:04,153
...he'll be deported.
He's at least fired. Might be arrested.

147
00:15:11,201 --> 00:15:14,121
Hey, hold up. Wait!

148
00:15:14,705 --> 00:15:15,664
Hold on.

149
00:15:16,165 --> 00:15:17,416
Goddamn.

150
00:15:17,917 --> 00:15:20,210
What are you doing?

151
00:15:22,546 --> 00:15:25,215
You knew you'd be found out.

152
00:15:27,801 --> 00:15:28,802
So what?

153
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:35,392
You told me about that case so I'd keep
going with the investigation. Right?

154
00:15:38,771 --> 00:15:40,272
I just wanted to catch him.

155
00:15:41,106 --> 00:15:42,066
The killer.

156
00:15:43,943 --> 00:15:45,402
Before he kills again.

157
00:15:48,489 --> 00:15:49,990
I'll find him, okay?

158
00:15:50,532 --> 00:15:51,492
I promise.

159
00:15:53,285 --> 00:15:54,870
I'll be sure to bring him in.

160
00:16:09,468 --> 00:16:11,470
I apologize for the trouble I've caused.

161
00:16:16,016 --> 00:16:17,017
Huh?

162
00:16:29,363 --> 00:16:31,865
We're taking you directly to the airport.

163
00:16:32,533 --> 00:16:35,035
Your personal items
will be sent over later.

164
00:16:41,125 --> 00:16:43,668
DETECTIVE, I FOUND THE SOURCE
OF THE PHRASE AT THE SCENE

165
00:16:43,669 --> 00:16:45,713
FROM: PROF. JANG
(DEPT. OF CRIMINAL HISTORY)

166
00:18:45,833 --> 00:18:48,835
"ONE FEAR BREEDS INFINITE FEAR

167
00:18:48,836 --> 00:18:52,380
AND ALL METHODS
SHALL BE CONCEALED."

168
00:18:52,381 --> 00:18:54,715
THE TALE OF EMIRI,
HIDDEN HERO OF THE IMJIN WAR - 3

169
00:18:54,716 --> 00:18:56,175
POST PUBLISHED

170
00:18:56,176 --> 00:18:59,346
SEOUL METROPOLITAN POLICE AGENCY

171
00:19:06,061 --> 00:19:08,563
MOBILE INVESTIGATION UNIT

172
00:19:08,564 --> 00:19:10,148
Come on. Let's go!

173
00:19:10,149 --> 00:19:11,691
- It's hard.
- You scared?

174
00:19:11,692 --> 00:19:13,944
Come on, get up.
Get up, you wimp! Let's go.

175
00:19:15,696 --> 00:19:17,948
Hey, you back?
Can you get that file for me?

176
00:19:22,828 --> 00:19:24,537
- Eat up.
- All right, fellas.

177
00:19:24,538 --> 00:19:25,705
Stuff your faces.

178
00:19:25,706 --> 00:19:27,415
Hey, hey, where's our thank-you?

179
00:19:27,416 --> 00:19:29,251
Oh, thank you for the food.

180
00:19:32,546 --> 00:19:34,464
You like it? Here.

181
00:19:35,549 --> 00:19:38,302
You want some more? Hey, you, uh...

182
00:19:56,778 --> 00:19:57,946
Hey!

183
00:20:10,584 --> 00:20:13,629
If it isn't the bastard
who just makes my job harder.

184
00:20:14,755 --> 00:20:17,132
I already knew
you were a crazy piece of shit.

185
00:20:17,716 --> 00:20:20,092
But I didn't know you'd go
and embarrass our entire country.

186
00:20:20,093 --> 00:20:21,720
And in Japan, no less.

187
00:20:23,764 --> 00:20:26,850
The World Cup got you?
You think you're better than us?

188
00:20:27,851 --> 00:20:31,146
You think your track record
makes you untouchable? Is that it?

189
00:20:32,022 --> 00:20:33,106
And what if...

190
00:20:35,067 --> 00:20:37,110
I let you take
some of the credit for this one?

191
00:20:38,278 --> 00:20:39,571
What?

192
00:20:40,239 --> 00:20:42,449
Interested?

193
00:20:43,242 --> 00:20:45,244
Ever heard of "mutual benefit?"

194
00:20:46,245 --> 00:20:47,955
Oh, Inspector Kim.

195
00:20:50,374 --> 00:20:52,376
You think I was born yesterday.
Is that it?

196
00:20:53,293 --> 00:20:55,461
"Mutual benefit," my ass.

197
00:20:55,462 --> 00:20:56,838
Try me again.

198
00:20:57,422 --> 00:20:59,174
Come on, bastard. Try that again!

199
00:21:00,300 --> 00:21:01,301
Chief Gu.

200
00:21:01,927 --> 00:21:04,471
Uh, sorry to interrupt,

201
00:21:04,972 --> 00:21:07,390
but there's someone here
for Inspector Kim.

202
00:21:07,391 --> 00:21:08,558
Yeah? Who?

203
00:21:15,691 --> 00:21:16,692
Ah.

204
00:21:17,526 --> 00:21:19,695
- Ah.
- Can we help you?

205
00:21:22,364 --> 00:21:23,906
- Where am I?
- Who's that?

206
00:21:23,907 --> 00:21:25,450
You the yakuza?

207
00:21:28,370 --> 00:21:29,955
Aw, adorable.

208
00:21:31,248 --> 00:21:32,833
- Ah.
- The hell is this?

209
00:21:36,628 --> 00:21:37,462
You tell me.

210
00:21:38,213 --> 00:21:39,256
What?

211
00:21:49,057 --> 00:21:50,392
Oh, hey.

212
00:21:51,560 --> 00:21:52,686
'Sup, man?

213
00:22:01,278 --> 00:22:03,071
Strange choice of <i>bok-jang.</i>

214
00:22:04,323 --> 00:22:05,282
<i>Bok-jang?</i>

215
00:22:07,409 --> 00:22:09,369
- Yeah, it's odd.
- Hold on. One minute.

216
00:22:13,957 --> 00:22:16,917
ACHIEVE PERFECTION!
TRAIN YOUR KOREAN

217
00:22:16,918 --> 00:22:18,003
Oh, my T-shirt.

218
00:22:19,713 --> 00:22:22,132
Uh... yeah.

219
00:22:24,134 --> 00:22:25,344
You here to support us?

220
00:22:28,013 --> 00:22:30,891
Well, I'm here for work reasons,
unlike you.

221
00:22:36,980 --> 00:22:40,025
Seems like it's all been sorted out
with your boss too.

222
00:22:43,528 --> 00:22:46,198
You're saying this happened that fast?

223
00:22:46,698 --> 00:22:48,325
Well, yeah, obviously.

224
00:22:49,701 --> 00:22:52,329
Someone clearly wants this case solved.

225
00:22:53,288 --> 00:22:57,000
Either it's our guys or our buddy.

226
00:22:58,251 --> 00:23:00,003
The guy with the pachinko business?

227
00:23:00,712 --> 00:23:01,880
Seo Hyun-tae?

228
00:23:03,090 --> 00:23:04,257
Mm-hmm.

229
00:23:06,676 --> 00:23:09,178
It doesn't matter to me
who's responsible for this,

230
00:23:09,179 --> 00:23:11,306
just as long as I can catch the killer.

231
00:23:14,810 --> 00:23:16,269
Come on. Let's finish this.

232
00:23:20,273 --> 00:23:21,400
Let's finish this.

233
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:23,985
- I mean, that's what I just said.
- Mmm.

234
00:23:25,362 --> 00:23:28,740
- "Let's finish this." This guy.
- Right. Come with me. We'll meet someone.

235
00:23:34,204 --> 00:23:35,871
Some kimchi for you?

236
00:23:35,872 --> 00:23:37,123
Need anything else?

237
00:23:37,124 --> 00:23:39,667
Hi, Professor.
Thank you for taking the time to meet us.

238
00:23:39,668 --> 00:23:41,795
Oh, it's my pleasure. Here.

239
00:23:44,756 --> 00:23:47,217
- Thank you, sir.
- Yes. Cheers.

240
00:23:48,635 --> 00:23:49,594
Go ahead.

241
00:23:53,014 --> 00:23:58,352
I've already filled the others in about
that phrase you asked me about earlier.

242
00:23:58,353 --> 00:24:00,729
Now, according to our historical records,

243
00:24:00,730 --> 00:24:03,649
there was a general during the Imjin War
named Emiri,

244
00:24:03,650 --> 00:24:05,693
who naturalized
to fight in the Joseon army.

245
00:24:05,694 --> 00:24:09,155
And... look at this painting here.

246
00:24:09,156 --> 00:24:12,324
Emiri said that for the Joseon army
to come to victory,

247
00:24:12,325 --> 00:24:14,578
it was important
that they destroy Japan's morale.

248
00:24:15,954 --> 00:24:18,331
And so he had
the enemies' corpses displayed.

249
00:24:19,833 --> 00:24:21,585
Displayed... Oh.

250
00:24:24,838 --> 00:24:25,839
Mmm...

251
00:24:26,548 --> 00:24:29,091
- Look here.
- Uh-huh.

252
00:24:29,092 --> 00:24:31,051
"One fear gives birth to endless fears,

253
00:24:31,052 --> 00:24:32,888
but its methods shall remain hidden."

254
00:24:33,889 --> 00:24:37,350
Yes, so you said this phrase
was left at the crime scene?

255
00:24:38,059 --> 00:24:40,937
<i>It comes from a book Emiri wrote
on military strategy.</i>

256
00:24:42,981 --> 00:24:45,774
Emiri was a general
in the Joseon army when he wrote it.

257
00:24:45,775 --> 00:24:48,695
He was widely known
for his particularly brutal methods.

258
00:24:53,450 --> 00:24:56,077
<i>He would use poppies
to drug the Japanese soldiers...</i>

259
00:24:58,705 --> 00:24:59,747
<i>...tie them to racks,</i>

260
00:24:59,748 --> 00:25:01,958
<i>and would break their limbs
to twist them around.</i>

261
00:25:05,504 --> 00:25:08,423
<i>He would then
display their disfigured corpses,</i>

262
00:25:09,132 --> 00:25:12,219
<i>the phrase painted in blood
right beneath them.</i>

263
00:25:13,929 --> 00:25:17,474
<i>Fear spread far and wide
among the enemy ranks.</i>

264
00:25:20,227 --> 00:25:23,020
The story dates
all the way back to the Joseon dynasty.

265
00:25:23,021 --> 00:25:25,690
To think it's being recreated now,
it's astonishing.

266
00:25:28,610 --> 00:25:30,695
Something to consider.

267
00:25:31,947 --> 00:25:33,572
If Emiri did exist in real life,

268
00:25:33,573 --> 00:25:36,201
could it be that his descendants
are still around today?

269
00:25:37,410 --> 00:25:40,955
The Imjin War made things very difficult
for the Japanese soldiers

270
00:25:40,956 --> 00:25:43,165
who continued to live in Joseon
after surrendering.

271
00:25:43,166 --> 00:25:45,834
Most of them remained,
but their lives weren't easy.

272
00:25:45,835 --> 00:25:48,379
Some accounts say
it was far worse than expected.

273
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:49,505
Mmm.

274
00:25:49,506 --> 00:25:52,258
There's even a theory
that Emiri was actually betrayed

275
00:25:52,259 --> 00:25:55,094
and killed by another soldier
who had also remained.

276
00:25:55,095 --> 00:25:57,681
Betrayed? And... And killed?

277
00:25:58,807 --> 00:26:01,559
The full story is
that he was killed by a Japanese spy

278
00:26:01,560 --> 00:26:05,105
that had been sent over
to gather intel for the next war.

279
00:26:06,690 --> 00:26:08,358
Killed by a subordinate.

280
00:26:09,859 --> 00:26:11,277
What's even more interesting

281
00:26:11,278 --> 00:26:14,488
is that there are barely any records
of Emiri here in Korea,

282
00:26:14,489 --> 00:26:17,283
but there are some in Japan
that describe him as a god,

283
00:26:17,284 --> 00:26:20,161
written by Japanese soldiers
who were worshippers.

284
00:26:20,829 --> 00:26:23,247
After all,
Japan has their fair share of gods.

285
00:26:23,248 --> 00:26:27,544
There's even evidence General Yi Sun-sin
was worshipped as a god of war.

286
00:26:29,588 --> 00:26:32,716
So there were people
who considered Emiri to be a god.

287
00:26:36,303 --> 00:26:38,304
Combining investigations?

288
00:26:38,305 --> 00:26:40,015
Between Korea and Japan?

289
00:26:41,099 --> 00:26:42,309
Ah, damn it!

290
00:26:43,393 --> 00:26:44,603
Chief!

291
00:26:45,604 --> 00:26:46,605
Chief!

292
00:26:47,188 --> 00:26:49,982
Check out this post. It was written
the same week Park In-gyu died.

293
00:26:49,983 --> 00:26:51,358
This is a bad time, buddy.

294
00:26:51,359 --> 00:26:54,194
Chief, this post is suspicious.
Trust me, you should take a look at it.

295
00:26:54,195 --> 00:26:55,696
Spit it out!

296
00:26:55,697 --> 00:26:56,739
It's, uh...

297
00:26:56,740 --> 00:27:00,034
It's about that phrase from the Imjin War,
the one at the crime scene.

298
00:27:00,035 --> 00:27:03,079
It's about the... the origin
that Professor Jang mentioned.

299
00:27:03,622 --> 00:27:04,623
You know the guy.

300
00:27:05,415 --> 00:27:08,459
- The one that Kim Seok...
- What? What?

301
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:10,836
Just 'cause a guy
uploads some stupid essay

302
00:27:10,837 --> 00:27:14,048
about Joseon history on a random site,
not even the same day but the week,

303
00:27:14,049 --> 00:27:16,008
just like that
you think you caught the killer?

304
00:27:16,009 --> 00:27:17,344
That what you think?

305
00:27:18,261 --> 00:27:19,303
You idiot.

306
00:27:19,304 --> 00:27:22,097
Okay, I'm sorry.
I'll bring it to Detective Kim Seok--

307
00:27:22,098 --> 00:27:24,267
What did you say? You little... Hey.

308
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:29,105
Go find the IP address.

309
00:27:32,233 --> 00:27:34,402
- And who do you work for?
- For you, sir.

310
00:27:35,779 --> 00:27:38,322
Forgive me. Have a nice day.

311
00:27:38,323 --> 00:27:39,782
Goddamn Inspector Kim.

312
00:27:39,783 --> 00:27:40,909
Motherfucker.

313
00:27:46,122 --> 00:27:47,247
CYBER CRIMES UNIT

314
00:27:47,248 --> 00:27:50,084
Track this IP address.

315
00:27:50,085 --> 00:27:52,336
MESSAGE SENT

316
00:27:52,337 --> 00:27:55,798
If you need anything else,
please feel free to reach out at anytime.

317
00:27:55,799 --> 00:27:57,341
- Thank you, Professor.
- Great.

318
00:27:57,342 --> 00:27:58,843
- Thank you.
- My pleasure.

319
00:28:02,555 --> 00:28:04,933
That's a sad story.

320
00:28:13,316 --> 00:28:14,858
What's so sad about it?

321
00:28:14,859 --> 00:28:17,444
Well, this Emiri guy had a tragic life.

322
00:28:17,445 --> 00:28:20,406
He ended up betrayed by his own underling.

323
00:28:20,407 --> 00:28:23,785
Well, you guys started the war.
Only got yourselves to blame, yeah?

324
00:28:25,453 --> 00:28:27,205
That's not very fair, man.

325
00:28:29,457 --> 00:28:33,128
I came to Korea so I could help.
That's it.

326
00:28:34,879 --> 00:28:36,338
So you could help?

327
00:28:36,339 --> 00:28:37,340
Yeah.

328
00:28:38,133 --> 00:28:39,384
You're helping me?

329
00:28:42,220 --> 00:28:43,930
You and I are doing this together.

330
00:28:44,431 --> 00:28:46,599
Better not forget that, buddy. Okay?

331
